WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:05.860
We've just reached an entire new milestone
in space exploration. "A mission to go to

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:11.700
a comet, drill out a sample, and bring it
back to Earth." The European Space Agency's

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.509
Rosetta spacecraft just landed a small, 220-pound
robot onto a comet, making it the 7th celestial

00:00:17.509 --> 00:00:22.690
body we've ever landed a spacecraft on. Previous
missions have flown by comets, and in 2005,

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:27.380
NASA intentionally crashed a spacecraft into
a comet to analyze its interior, but this

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.789
is the first mission to make a controlled
landing on one. Comets are frigid chunks of

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:35.329
ice and rock that orbit the sun. They're a
bit like asteroids, except they have more

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:39.319
elliptical orbits that sometimes take them
so close to the sun that they start to vaporize,

00:00:39.319 --> 00:00:43.159
creating the bright tails they're known for.
Scientists think comets may have brought water

00:00:43.159 --> 00:00:47.870
and complex organic molecules to Earth, which
we can thank for our existence. The comet

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:53.389
in question is called 67P/C-G, named after
the Ukrainian astronomers who first spotted

00:00:53.389 --> 00:00:58.119
it in 1969. It's sort of duck shaped and about
2.5 miles wide. Picture a rock the size of

00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:02.909
Japan's Mt. Fuji, rotating every 12 hours
and moving through space at about 84,000 miles

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:07.960
per hour, millions of miles away from Earth.
Comet 67P orbits the sun every 6 and a half

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.229
years from a point just beyond Jupiter. To
reach it, Rosetta had to loop around several

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:16.509
times, passing by Earth and Mars to use their
gravity as a slingshot. As a result, it's

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:23.509
been traveling for more than a decade. It
left Earth way back in March 2004 and has

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.200
traveled over 3.5 billion miles to reach the
comet. That's nearly 40 times the distance

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.520
from the Earth to the sun. As it traveled
too far to be powered by the sun, the spacecraft

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.579
went into hibernation for 2.5 years on the
way there. Rosetta woke up at the beginning

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:40.789
of this year and caught up with the comet
in August. Just putting the probe in orbit

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:44.369
around the comet was a major feat -- we'd
never done that before -- especially since

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:49.069
engineers expected a potato-shaped comet,
and ended up with a duck. Last month, scientists

00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:53.140
picked out a site for the lander, a nice smooth
flat spot on the comet's smaller lobe. The

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.369
lander flew down to the comet over the course
of several hours, slowing itself down and

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:02.039
eventually landing at the speed of a person
walking. "Here we have very very low gravity.

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:10.919
So this lander, which has a mass of about
100kg, will have the weight of about a sheet

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.950
of paper when it comes down on the comet.
The problem is to stay there." After 10 years

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.239
of flight, the lander has an incredibly short
amount of time to conduct science on the comet's

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:25.730
surface: 64 hours. That's how long its battery
lasts, and that's how long it has to drill

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:30.080
into the comet, collect soil samples, and
chemically analyze them. After that, the lander

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.840
deactivates unless it can get enough solar
power to recharge. But Rosetta will continue

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.250
to orbit the comet and keep sending back photos
and other data until December 2015, when the

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.200
comet heads back out of the inner solar system.
All this research will tell us more about

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.319
the composition of comets than we've ever
known before. And because comets are the oldest

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:50.510
things we can reach, this information could
help us better understand the very beginnings

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.890
of our solar system.

